# Confluence tables: always full width

`Last updated: 2026-06-22.`

When creating or editing any table inside a Confluence page (via the Atlassian MCP
`createConfluencePage` / `updateConfluencePage`, or any other path), the table must
render at **full page width**, not the default constrained width.

## How

- In the HTML / storage body you send, set `data-layout="full-width"` on every
  `<table>` element:

```html
<table data-layout="full-width">
  ...
</table>
```

- Do **not** use `data-layout="default"` (page-width constrained) or
  `data-layout="center"` for content tables. `full-width` is the default for all
  tables unless the user explicitly asks for a narrow / centered table.

## Notes

- Confluence storage format stores this as the `data-layout` attribute on the
  table; full-width makes the table span the whole content area (wide-mode).
- If a table was already created without it, set `data-layout="full-width"` when
  next editing the page.
- Still set `data-layout="full-width"` on every table, including tables nested
  inside expand macros / panels / layout columns.

## Caveat: full-width only breaks out for top-level tables

`full-width` only renders edge-to-edge for tables at the **top level** of the page
body. A table nested inside an **expand macro**, **panel**, or **layout column** is
capped at that container's width, so the attribute has no visible effect there.
This is a Confluence platform constraint, not a markup error.

- If a table must be visually full-width, place it at the top level (outside any
  expand/panel/column), e.g. under a plain heading.
- If collapsible/expand sections are required, accept that their nested tables
  render at the container width. Do not keep re-editing trying to force it wider;
  explain the tradeoff to the user instead.
